---------- Statistical -------------------

Overall: What model do we use?
- form2/m2: form2 <- bf(ystd ~   0 + tx_sex + tx_sex:time  + (1 + time|participant) +  (1|participant:leg))
  - No intercept, tx_sex fixed effect - all 10 groups (5 tx * 2 sex) get fully independent, unpooled intercepts
  - tx_sex:time: each of the 10 groups gets its own fully independent pre-post change, with no shrinkage toward a grand mean
  - Particiant-level random intercept and random time slope (individual trajectories), plus participant:leg
  
- form3/m3: form3 <- bf(ystd ~   time + allocation + allocation:time +  (1 + time|participant) +  (1|participant:leg) + (time | tx_sex))
  - Same fixed-effect structure, distinguishing between allocation only (intervention, control)
  - Age and volume-conditions arent fixed effects here
  - Group granularity via varying tx_sex effect
  - Participant gets the fuller random structure like form2
  
- Strenght: Model both reps at each time point (1x famil and 1x baseline, 2x mid, 2x post)? All leg strength into one model? 


Muscle Thickness - RESOLVED
- Look at both V. lateralis and intermedius
    - likely only lateralis, since theres usually not much happening in intermedius (take it separately if anything)
- 3x pictures per leg/time point - average or use all three?
    - Try all three, perhaps less noise than averaging them all
- UPDATE: No meaningful change after including all three images at each measurement. Some contrasts came 
out slightly narrower, some slightly wider, with no systemaci change in precision. The same contrasts
remain credible (clearing the 95% CI in both) and not credible. Based on that, it seems best to keep the
original modelling as is, and keep the "all images" one as supplementary, should anyone ask about it. 


